  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{29\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{29\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{21\\pi }{5}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{21\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{29\\pi }{5}.$",
